WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
 So, as you can see, none of this is easy. It wasn't easy for Larry Smarr, and now we basically have the lobbyists once again.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:18.000
 The lobbyists are deciding that there's only going to be $15 million, because if it's $15 million, they can only build a 56 kilobit line.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:26.000
 But Michigan thought outside the box. Some people might say we cheated. Other universities wanted to get the grant, kind of thought we did cheat.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:32.000
 We didn't play by the rules. Like in Star Trek, we rewrote the rules. So we got it.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:41.000
 And we slid underneath the nose of AT&T, a 1.5 megabit network that started large and started fast and got faster.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:50.000
 And as Doug talked, it was a very, very, very successful network. And it had follow-on effects, just like Larry talked about.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:57.000
 It had these follow-on effects of causing campuses to build networking, because in would come this really fast national network.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:03.000
 So you better fix the on-campus networking. So it was really quite wonderful. And partners, IBM, MCI.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:09.000
 The other thing is AT&T was being beat up by MCI at the time, and maybe they weren't noticing.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:15.000
 So we might have been lucky. It could easily have been two years earlier or two years later. It might not have worked.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.000
 But whatever. It did work. And we can be very grateful.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:28.000
 So the University of Michigan was sort of the center of -- they ran the Network Operations Center and did the network design.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:36.000
 Worked on the software with IBM and built the network. And it had a very exponential growth as it went forward.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:44.000
 This building right here is on our north campus. And it was, for me personally, the very first place that I typed the first characters that went across the internet.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.000
 I walked in this door to -- I wasn't at the university at that time, but I had friends here.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:54.000
 And so I came in and I was typing on, you know, "Hello" on the internet.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:02.000
 And so this building was where the Network Operations Center was and where I touched the internet for the -- the NSF net for the first time.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:10.000
 So it started out real simple, but its goal -- its goal was to enable connections to other networks.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.000
 The regional networks got formed. Larry Smarr mentioned that.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:21.000
 And so then the backbone became sort of a forcing function to get more campuses on and more and more states, et cetera, et cetera.

